{
  "$comment": "Conformance vectors for whole exchanges, normative in kernel/specs/controller-protocol.md §11. Every table here is normative. Each row is one exchange on one carrier instance, `open` naming the sessions already open when it begins. A frame's `frame` is its bytes in lowercase hex and MUST decode to its `meta` and `data`. A row asserts: every payload satisfies its message's schema; the kernel end mints even ids and the controller end odd ones (§3.2), so a frame's id alone says whether it is a request or a response; every response answers an outstanding request of the same id, session and type; Session.Hello carries no session and nothing else carries none, and every other frame names a session that is open; a message whose `response` is null draws no response frame; a synchronous request's sender sends nothing but responses while it is outstanding; a channel's chunks sit between its Open and Close with contiguous seq and within the credit granted to it; an error answer names a code its message declares. `reentrancy` names the outer synchronous request and the inner request served while it is outstanding; `signal` names a code raised by one message and re-raised unchanged by another, which is what a signal is.",
